  "writeup_markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Bishareen\"\nsubtitle: \"Sudan\"\nregion: \"Sub Saharan Africa\"\ntags: [ethnography, sub-saharan-africa]\n---\n\n## At a glance\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Who | Bishareen people |\n| Where | Sudan |\n| How many | Not covered by the sources |\n| Language | Not covered by the sources |\n| Religion | Not covered by the sources |\n| Known for | Beadwork and shell aprons · Straw caps · Basketry hats · Hide shields · Baskets and gourd containers |\n\n## Overview\n\nThe sources give little broad information about the Bishareen people. Museum records mainly document clothing, baskets, containers, weapons, and a shield. A photograph shows a man standing before a domed shelter covered with mats.\n\n## Material culture\n\nThe documented material culture includes beadwork, shells, straw, leather, fibre, grass, gourds, iron, silver, and wood.\n\n### Clothing & dress\n\nThe records show decorated aprons, straw caps, and a conical basketry hat.\n\n- **Openwork woman’s apron** — This apron is made of glass beadwork and shells with a skin fringe.\n- **Cowrie-shell apron** — This apron is made of cowrie shells and glass beads.\n- **Pointed straw caps** — These caps are made of coloured straws.\n- **Conical basketry hat** — This hat has green and navy blue bands with yellow and red decorations.\n\n### Architecture\n\nA photograph shows a domed shelter covered with mats.\n\n- **Domed shelter** — An adult male stands in front of a domed shelter covered with mats.\n\n### Ceramics, metalwork & everyday objects\n\nThe records include a hide shield, a spear, gourd containers, and several kinds of baskets.\n\n- **Hippopotamus-hide shield** — The shield has a central silver boss, silver plates in the rim, and eight leather pockets containing charms.\n- **Spear or lance** — The weapon has an iron head and a wooden shaft.\n- **Beaded gourd containers** — The gourd containers are decorated with beads or covered with a network of beads.\n- **Leather-strip and fibre baskets** — These baskets are made from leather strips and fibre.\n- **Coiled grass or fibre baskets** — These baskets are flat or conical and are formed of coiled grass or fibre.\n\n## Foodways\n\nThe sources do not cover Bishareen foodways, but one record identifies a possible food-cover.\n\n- **Possible food-cover** — One catalogue record identifies an object as a possible food-cover.\n\n## Language & religion\n\n- **Shield charms** — A catalogue record describes leather pockets containing charms attached to a shield.\n\n## Sources & further reading\n\n- The supplied sources contain no books, named scholars, or documentation projects.\n- No Wikipedia article was supplied.\n- No UNESCO Intangible Cultural Heritage inscription was supplied.\n- The relevant supplied museum source is the British Museum catalogue, represented by records for a shield, spear, aprons, hats, caps, baskets, containers, food-covers, and photographic prints.\n",
